{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 29, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 and Prince of Galilee and Tiberias from 1174 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond III was born around 1140. He succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152 at the age of 12. His mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III's early reign was marked by conflicts with the Byzantine Empire and the Fatimid Caliphate. In 1164 he was captured by the Fatimids while on a raid in the Bekaa Valley. He was held captive in Cairo for a year before being released.\n\n*Marriage and Alliances*\nIn 1174 Raymond III married Eschiva of Bures Princess of Galilee and Tiberias. The marriage brought him control of the Principality of Galilee and Tiberias which he held until his death.\n\nRaymond III formed an alliance with Saladin the Ayyubid Sultan of Egypt and Syria in 1174. The alliance was formed in opposition to the growing power of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. However the alliance was short-lived and Raymond III soon found himself at odds with Saladin.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Later Life*\nIn 1187 Raymond III played a key role in the Battle of Hattin. The battle was a decisive victory for Saladin and marked the beginning of the end of the Crusader states in the Holy Land. Raymond III was one of the few Crusader leaders to escape the battle.\n\nAfter the Battle of Hattin Raymond III fled to the city of Tripoli which he held until his death in 1187. He died childless and was succeeded by his cousin Raymond IV of Tripoli.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.004263356793671846}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -6.318112355074845e-06}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.35265472531318665}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Tr